<div class="page">

  <header>
    <p class="eyebrow">Progress report · proximity skin for robot arms</p>
    <h1>Feeling in the dark</h1>
    <p class="byline"><span>Jay Vakil</span><span>24 July – 14 August 2026</span><span>weekly from here</span></p>
    <p class="lede">The main result held up and is figure-ready: a proximity skin cuts crashes from
      <strong>66 out of 100 tries down to 40</strong> when the cameras cannot see the obstacle. The last
      three weeks went into a follow-up experiment that <span class="no">did not work</span> — and the
      reason it failed changed how I plan to run the next one.</p>

    <div class="kpis">
      <div class="kpi">
        <span class="kpi-val">66% &rarr; 40%</span>
        <span class="kpi-lab">Crashes, with the obstacle hidden from the cameras</span>
        <span class="kpi-sub">p = 0.016 · 50 tries</span>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi">
        <span class="kpi-val">225 tries</span>
        <span class="kpi-lab">Blur follow-up: 13.4 hours of GPU, no usable pattern</span>
        <span class="kpi-sub">negative result</span>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi">
        <span class="kpi-val">&plusmn;40 pts</span>
        <span class="kpi-lab">How much of a 25-try number is luck. Now measured.</span>
        <span class="kpi-sub">50 tries is the new floor</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="shead"><span class="snum">01</span><h2>What we build, in one picture</h2></div>
    <figure class="arm">
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:00_arm.png}}" alt="Simulated Franka arm covered in blue sensor pads with red sensor dots"></div>
      <figcaption>40 small distance sensors (red dots) spread over the whole arm. Each answers only one
        question — <b>how close is something, right here?</b> No colour, no shape. Think 40 parking
        sensors. A camera cannot see past the arm's own body; these can.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="col">Every robot is tested in three setups: <b>no obstacle</b>, an obstacle the cameras
      <b>can</b> see, and an obstacle the cameras <b>cannot</b> see. We hide it from the camera renderer
      only — the physics is unchanged and the skin still feels it. That last setup is the clean version
      of an everyday failure: fog, dim light, a dirty lens, an obstacle the same colour as the wall.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="shead"><span class="snum">02</span><h2>The result that matters</h2></div>
    <figure>
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:01_main_result.png}}" alt="Grouped bar charts of crash rate and task success for cameras only versus cameras plus skin"></div>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:02_graded_benefit.png}}" alt="Bar chart: skin benefit is 2 points with no bar, 14 points with a visible bar, 26 points with a hidden bar"></div>
      <figcaption>The benefit lines up with how blind the cameras are: <b>2 points, then 14, then
        26</b>. Luck does not sort itself into an order like that. This is the strongest evidence in
        the project and it is new this week.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="col">One more thing worth flagging: the camera-only robot crashes just as much when the
      obstacle is <b>fully visible</b> to it (64% vs 66%). It never learned obstacle avoidance from the
      cameras at all. So the skin is not a backup sense here — it is the only sense doing avoidance.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="shead"><span class="snum">03</span><h2>What I did in these three weeks</h2></div>
    <figure>
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:07_timeline.png}}" alt="Timeline of the three weeks from 24 July to 14 August"></div>
    </figure>
    <p class="col">The plan was simple: if the story is <em>“the skin matters because cameras fail,”</em>
      then break the cameras and watch what happens. I trained three camera-only robots with every
      training image blurred, then tested them on clear images.</p>
    <figure>
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:08_what_blur_looks_like.png}}" alt="Four camera frames at increasing blur, room camera on top and wrist camera below"></div>
      <figcaption>What the robot saw while training. Left to right: no blur, then increasing blur. Top
        row is the room camera, bottom row the wrist camera.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:03_blur_no_pattern.png}}" alt="Bar charts showing task success and crash rate across blur levels with no orderly pattern"></div>
      <figcaption>225 test runs, 13.4 hours, and <b>no usable pattern in either direction</b>. Mild
        blur wipes the robot out; more blur brings it back.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="shead"><span class="snum">04</span><h2>Lessons learned</h2></div>
    <p class="col">This is the useful part of the three weeks.</p>

    <div class="col">
      <div class="lesson bad">
        <span class="tag">Lesson 1 · measurement</span>
        <h3>A low crash rate can mean “broken”, not “careful”.</h3>
        <p>The mildly-blurred robot has the fewest crashes of anything I have trained. It also almost
          never finishes the task — it learned to barely move. Only the strict score, <b>did the job AND
          never touched anything</b>, tells careful apart from broken.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:05_low_crashes_not_safe.png}}" alt="Bar chart contrasting crash rate against strict success for each blur level"></div>
    </figure>

    <div class="col">
      <div class="lesson bad">
        <span class="tag">Lesson 2 · what to trust</span>
        <h3>Training numbers did not predict what the robot did.</h3>
        <p>The prediction errors formed a perfect ladder. The behaviour didn't. The robot with the
          second-worst score on paper was the best robot in the room.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:06_training_error_lied.png}}" alt="Two panels: training error rises in a clean ladder while task success zig-zags"></div>
    </figure>

    <div class="col">
      <div class="lesson bad">
        <span class="tag">Lesson 3 · how much is luck</span>
        <h3>25 tries is not enough — and now I know by how much.</h3>
        <p>Below is one robot in three setups that differ only in whether an obstacle exists. Blur cannot
          cause that gap, so <b>40 points is what luck looks like at 25 tries</b>. Everything from here
          uses 50 minimum.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <div class="plate"><img src="{{IMG:04_noise_proof.png}}" alt="Bar chart: same brain, 28 percent crashes with no bar versus 68 percent in both bar setups"></div>
    </figure>

    <div class="col">
      <div class="lesson">
        <span class="tag">Lesson 4 · process</span>
        <h3>Two habits, each already paid for with a lost night.</h3>
        <p>A graphics-driver mismatch let all 25 runs fail silently overnight — there is now a two-second
          check that refuses to start. And a 13-hour job printed an empty summary table because of a
          one-line bug in the part that runs <em>last</em>. Test the summary step before launching the
          long part.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="shead"><span class="snum">05</span><h2>Numbers</h2></div>
    <div class="tw">
      <table>
        <caption>50 tries per number</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Measure</th><th>No obstacle</th><th>Obstacle visible</th><th>Obstacle hidden</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Crashes — cameras only</td><td class="num">60%</td><td class="num">64%</td><td class="num">66%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Crashes — cameras + skin</td><td class="num">58%</td><td class="num">50%</td><td class="num win">40%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Task success — cameras only</td><td class="num">22%</td><td class="num">28%</td><td class="num">36%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Task success — cameras + skin</td><td class="num">18%</td><td class="num">16%</td><td class="num">30%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">The 66% &rarr; 40% gap has p = 0.016. Every success difference is within noise (p &ge; 0.23).</p>

    <div class="tw">
      <table>
        <caption>Everything else</caption>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Skin sensors</td><td class="num">40, each an 8&times;8 depth patch</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Directions the skin senses vs the wrist camera</td><td class="num">83% vs 10%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Skin readings under blur and near-darkness</td><td class="num">identical, bit for bit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Training demonstrations collected</td><td class="num">105 (wanted 200)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Prediction error — skin / cameras only / processed reflex</td><td class="num"><span class="win">0.0595</span> / 0.0755 / 0.0830</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Test speed</td><td class="num">3.6 min per try &rarr; 3 h per 50-try number</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Measured luck at 25 tries</td><td class="num">&plusmn;40 points</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Blur experiment</td><td class="num">225 tries, 13.4 h, no pattern</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="shead"><span class="snum">06</span><h2>Progress towards a paper</h2></div>
    <ul class="col">
      <li><b>Two figures are done and real</b> — sections 02 and 04 above. The graded 2 / 14 / 26 pattern
        is the single strongest thing we have, and it is new this week.</li>
      <li><b>The headline figure is now defined:</b> two crash curves as the cameras get worse — one flat
        and low with the skin, one flat and high without — with task success collapsing underneath both.
        It answers the question a reviewer actually asks: <em>why add a skin if you already have
        cameras?</em> Because cameras have a failure mode the skin does not, and it isn't exotic.</li>
      <li><b>The tool for it was built and verified this week.</b> I can now blur the cameras at
        <em>test</em> time on a finished robot, leaving the skin untouched. Same robot, same test
        scenarios, only camera quality changes — which removes the exact weakness that killed the blur
        experiment. First answer costs 12 hours of GPU.</li>
      <li><b>Still missing before it is submittable:</b> more than one training run per result, and a
        crash counter that can tell “hit the obstacle” from “brushed the wall”.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="shead"><span class="snum">07</span><h2>Blockers</h2></div>
    <ol class="col">
      <li><b>The crash counter is too blunt.</b> It counts any contact with the environment. About 60 of
        those 66 points are the arm brushing the tight space it was told to reach into; the obstacle
        itself only adds about 5. I am measuring a small effect through a noisy instrument. The code
        already works out <em>what</em> it touched, then throws the name away — a five-line fix, but old
        runs can't be recovered, so it costs a re-run.</li>
      <li><b>One GPU, and tests can't run in parallel</b> (memory). At 3.6 minutes per try, one
        experiment is 12–47 hours. This is the real limit on how much I can support statistically.</li>
      <li><b>The dataset is stuck at 105 demonstrations</b> instead of 200 — three of four collection
        workers get killed for using too much memory. Fixable by running fewer, slower.</li>
      <li><b>Every result is a single training run.</b> Three seeds each would turn “promising” into
        “defensible”, and triples the GPU time.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="shead"><span class="snum">08</span><h2>Next week</h2></div>
    <ol class="col">
      <li>Fix the crash counter so crashes are split by what was actually hit, then re-run.</li>
      <li>Run the 12-hour version of the new experiment: two robots, clear vs blurred cameras at test
        time, 50 tries each. Enough to see whether the two crash curves separate at all.</li>
      <li>Start repeat runs with different random seeds on the main result.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="ask col">
      <span class="tag">One question for you</span>
      <p>Limited GPU means I can do <em>either</em> repeat-runs of the existing result <em>or</em> the new
        camera-corruption experiment first, not both. My instinct is the new experiment, because it turns
        one condition into a curve and that is the figure a paper needs — but repeat-runs are what make
        the existing number safe to publish. Which would you rather see first?</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>
</div>
